  {
    "id": "2046722205019668936",
    "text": "Who are the brilliant minds behind this work at @category_xyz ?\n\nAnother part of why Monad inspires such confidence in many legacy friends when I introduce them to it \n\nVictor Shoup\n Role:Researcher at Category Labs; Professor at NYU (Courant Institute).\n Education:Ph.D. in Computer Science from the University of Wisconsin–Madison (1989).\n Professional Background:One of the most influential figures in modern cryptography. He is best known for the Cramer–Shoup cryptosystem (the first practical public-key system provably secure against adaptive chosen ciphertext attack).\nPrevious Roles: Researcher at Offchain Labs (Arbitrum), DFINITY, and IBM Zurich Research Lab.\nKey Contributions: Author of the widely used NTL (Number Theory Library) in C++ and the textbook A Computational Introduction to Number Theory and Algebra. \n\nSourav Das\n Role: Senior Researcher at Category Labs.\n Education: Ph.D. in Computer Science from UIUC (University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign); Undergraduate degree from IIT Delhi. \n Professional Background: His work focuses on the intersection of consensus protocols, privacy, and scalability.\nKey Contributions: Recognized for his research in Byzantine Reliable Broadcast and asynchronous data dissemination. He was a 2022 Chainlink Labs Ph.D. Fellow and has published extensively in top-tier security conferences like ACM CCS and IEEE S&P.\n\nPeter Rindal\n Role: Researcher at Category Labs.\n Education: Ph.D. from Oregon State University (under Mike Rosulek).\n Professional Background: An expert in Secure Multi-Party Computation (MPC) and *lPrivate Set Intersection (PSI). \nPrevious Roles: Staff Research Scientist at Visa Research; Intern/Consultant at Microsoft Research. \nKey Contributions: Developed some of the world's fastest MPC protocols and toolchains, often improving state-of-the-art performance by orders of magnitude. His libraries (like libOTe) are industry standards for Oblivious Transfer.\n\nBabak Poorebrahim Gilkalaye\n Role: Researcher at Category Labs.\n Education: Ph.D. in Computer Science from the University of Missouri–Kansas City. \nProfessional Background: Specializes in cryptography and privacy-preserving machine learning.\nKey Contributions: His research focuses on Homomorphic Encryption (HE) and \"Blind Learning\"—facilitating secure access to decentralized data without moving it outside the owner's infrastructure. He has a strong background in biometric security and information authentication.\n\nAmit Agarwal\nAt Category Labs, Agarwal focuses on the design and analysis of cryptography-related features for the Monad blockchain. \nExpertise: His work involves Secure Multiparty Computation (MPC), zero-knowledge proofs (ZK), and threshold encryption.\nKey Publications: He has co-authored several technical papers for the lab, including \"Weighted Batched Threshold Encryption with Applications to Mempool Privacy\" and \"Time-Lock Encrypted Storage for Blockchains\".\nBackground: He holds a PhD in Computer Science from the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign and previously held research positions at Google, Aptos Labs, and PayPal.",
